.SH DESCRIPTION
The standard data transfer post operations \(en fi_send, fi_write,
fi_read, and their variants \(en are monolithic: a single call formats a
work request, hands it to the provider, and initiates it on the fabric.
The work request operations decompose that lifecycle into discrete
stages \(en prepare, modify, queue, and flush \(en so that formatting a
work request is separated in time and place from submitting it.
.PP
An application that knows its transfers ahead of time formats its work
requests once, at initialization, and repeats only the queue and flush
steps in its inner loop; a work request may be queued any number of
times.
An application that repeats a transfer with only a few fields changed,
for example a different destination address or remote buffer, updates
those fields in place with the fi_wr_modify_* calls rather than
formatting a new work request.
The fi_tx_flush, fi_recv_flush, and fi_trecv_flush calls then give a
well\-defined point at which queued work is initiated, including
operations the provider deferred under the FI_MORE flag.
.PP
Prepare is a control path operation; modify, queue, and flush are data
path operations.
The transport behavior of a work request is identical to that of the
corresponding fi_*msg call, so a prepare\-queue\-flush sequence and the
standard post calls are interchangeable.
.PP
Completed operations are reported to the user through one or more
completion queues associated with the endpoint, exactly as they are for
the standard post calls.
The context reported in a completion is the one supplied to the queue
call, not one carried in the work request.
See \f[CR]fi_cq\f[R](3) for completion event details.
.SS Capability
Work request support is advertised through the FI_WR endpoint
capability.
An application requests it in the \f[CR]caps\f[R] field of the fi_info
hints passed to fi_getinfo, and a provider that supports work requests
reports FI_WR in the returned fi_info.
FI_WR is a secondary capability, so a provider may report it even when
the application does not request it.
A provider that reports FI_WR implements the full work request interface
\(en fi_wr_prepare, all of the fi_wr_modify_* calls, and the
fi_wr_queue_* calls \(en and reports the required work request sizes in
fi_ep_attr::max_tx_wr_size and fi_ep_attr::max_rx_wr_size.
Queued work requests are initiated with the endpoint flush calls
fi_tx_flush, fi_recv_flush, and fi_trecv_flush, which are available
independently of FI_WR.
.SS Work Request
A work request is described on input by a \f[CR]struct fi_wr_attr\f[R]
and formatted into an \f[CR]fi_wr\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\f[B]struct\f[R] fi_wr_attr {
    \f[B]enum\f[R] fi_op_type                 op_type;

    \f[B]union\f[R] {
        \f[B]struct\f[R] fi_op_msg            *msg;
        \f[B]struct\f[R] fi_op_tagged         *tagged;
        \f[B]struct\f[R] fi_op_rma            *rma;
        \f[B]struct\f[R] fi_op_atomic         *atomic;
        \f[B]struct\f[R] fi_op_fetch_atomic   *fetch_atomic;
        \f[B]struct\f[R] fi_op_compare_atomic *compare_atomic;
    } op;
};

\f[B]typedef\f[R] void *fi_wr;
.EE
.PP
The op_type field selects which member of the op union is valid.
Each \f[CR]fi_op_*\f[R] descriptor carries the target endpoint, the
operation\(cqs message descriptor, and its flags; these are the same
descriptors used by \f[CR]struct fi_deferred_work\f[R] (see
\f[CR]fi_trigger\f[R](3)).
The fi_wr_attr, and everything it references, is input only and is not
accessed after fi_wr_prepare returns.
.PP
\f[CR]fi_wr\f[R] is an opaque handle, defined as \f[CR]void *\f[R].
Its contents are provider defined and its buffer size is reported by the
provider.
The application allocates the work request, backing it with a buffer of
at least fi_ep_attr::max_tx_wr_size bytes for a transmit operation or
fi_ep_attr::max_rx_wr_size bytes for a receive operation, and owns it
for its entire lifetime.
.PP
A single work request may be queued any number of times, including
concurrently from multiple threads or device work items, each supplying
its own context.
If the provider requires the FI_CONTEXT or FI_CONTEXT2 mode, the context
passed to the queue call must be a struct fi_context or struct
fi_context2 respectively, following the usual rules for those modes.
