.TP
\f[I]compare\f[R]
The compare buffers of a compare atomic operation, as passed to
fi_compare_atomicmsg.
.TP
\f[I]flags\f[R]
The operation flags, with the meaning they have for the corresponding
fi_*msg call; see FLAGS below.
.PP
Because the operation descriptors are shared with the deferred work
queue interface, two of their fields do not apply here.
The endpoint they reference must match the endpoint the call is issued
on; a mismatch returns \-FI_EINVAL.
Their per operation context, for example fi_msg::context, is ignored, as
the context of a queued operation is supplied by the queue call instead.
.SS fi_wr_prepare
The fi_wr_prepare call validates its inputs and formats a work request
for the operation described by \f[I]attr\f[R] into \f[I]wr\f[R],
updating \f[I]wr_len\f[R] to the size of the formatted request.
Prepare does not queue the request and does not consume endpoint
transmit resources.
.PP
On output \f[I]wr_len\f[R] is the size the provider actually used, which
may be less than fi_ep_attr::max_tx_wr_size or
fi_ep_attr::max_rx_wr_size.
The required size can depend on the operation and on its flags, so the
endpoint attribute reports the largest work request the endpoint can
produce while \f[I]wr_len\f[R] reports the size of this one.
.SS fi_wr_queue_tx / fi_wr_queue_recv / fi_wr_queue_trecv
The queue calls place a prepared work request on an endpoint queue,
associating it with \f[I]context\f[R].
The work request is taken by const pointer and is not retained by the
provider: once the call returns successfully, the application may modify
or free \f[I]wr\f[R] without affecting the queued operation.
.PP
Queueing is not guaranteed to initiate the operation.
To guarantee that previously queued work requests have been initiated,
an application must flush the queue they were placed on: fi_tx_flush for
work queued with fi_wr_queue_tx, fi_recv_flush for fi_wr_queue_recv, and
fi_trecv_flush for fi_wr_queue_trecv.
A provider is also allowed to initiate queued work on its own to make
forward progress, for example when its internal batch limit is reached.
.PP
The three calls differ in the queue the work request is placed on, not
in the operation it performs.
After prepare, the operation is part of the formatted work request, so
every transmit work request \(en FI_OP_SEND, FI_OP_TSEND, FI_OP_READ,
FI_OP_WRITE, and the atomic operations \(en is queued with
fi_wr_queue_tx.
FI_OP_RECV work requests are queued with fi_wr_queue_recv and
FI_OP_TRECV work requests with fi_wr_queue_trecv, because a provider may
place untagged and tagged receives on different structures, for example
a hardware receive queue versus a posted tag matching list.
An application must queue a work request with the call that matches the
direction and operation it was prepared for.
.PP
Operation ordering, as given by the endpoint\(cqs msg_order attribute,
follows the order in which work requests are queued, not the order in
which they were prepared.
.SS Modifying a Work Request
Each fi_wr_modify_* call updates an already prepared work request,
leaving the rest of it formatted.
The following applies to all of them.
.PP
A field may be modified while operations queued from the same work
request are still outstanding; the change applies only to subsequent
queue calls.
.PP
The modify calls format in place and are not thread safe with respect to
any other call on the same work request.
An application must serialize those calls.
.SS fi_wr_modify_addr
The fi_wr_modify_addr call sets the peer address: the destination for a
transmit work request, or the source to match for a receive work
request.
\f[I]addr\f[R] is an address inserted in the endpoint\(cqs address
vector, or FI_ADDR_UNSPEC on a receive work request to accept any
source.
.SS fi_wr_modify_iov
The fi_wr_modify_iov call sets the local buffers and their memory
descriptors, and with them the length of the transfer.
For a fetching atomic work request this sets the operand buffers, not
the result buffer.
.PP
Length is a single property of the work request rather than one field of
each side of the transfer, and this is the call that owns it; see
fi_wr_modify_rma_iov below.
.PP
\f[I]count\f[R] must not exceed the count used at prepare, since
fi_wr_prepare may have sized the work request for that count.
A smaller value is always valid, so an application whose transfers vary
in segment count prepares once at its maximum and reduces the count per
transfer.
.SS fi_wr_modify_rma_iov
The fi_wr_modify_rma_iov call sets the remote buffers \(en their
addresses and keys.
It applies only to RMA and atomic work requests, and returns \-FI_EINVAL
otherwise.
.PP
The \f[CR]len\f[R] field of each fi_rma_iov is not used to change the
transfer length.
A value that does not agree with the current local length returns
\-FI_EINVAL.
.PP
\f[I]count\f[R] is bounded exactly as it is for fi_wr_modify_iov.
.SS fi_wr_modify_tag
The fi_wr_modify_tag call sets the tag, and for a tagged receive work
request the ignore bits.
It applies only to tagged work requests, and returns \-FI_EINVAL
otherwise.
\f[I]ignore\f[R] is unused for FI_OP_TSEND and must be 0.
.SS fi_wr_modify_data
The fi_wr_modify_data call sets the remote CQ data delivered with the
operation.
It applies only to a work request prepared with FI_REMOTE_CQ_DATA in its
operation flags, since that flag determines whether the formatted work
request carries a data field at all, and returns \-FI_EINVAL otherwise.
.SS fi_wr_modify_flags
The fi_wr_modify_flags call replaces the operation flags of a prepared
work request with \f[I]flags\f[R].
.PP
The flags that select the layout or size of the formatted work request
(e.g.
FI_REMOTE_CQ_DATA and FI_INJECT) cannot be changed, because doing so
would require reformatting the work request.
Such a flag returns \-FI_EINVAL when it conflicts with the prepared work
request, and is accepted when it agrees with it.
An application that needs the layout to change should prepare a new work
request.
.PP
A provider returns \-FI_EBADFLAGS for a flag whose effect it cannot
represent in the formatted work request.
.PP
The operation type is fixed at prepare and is not a flag.
fi_wr_modify_flags cannot change it.
.SH FLAGS
The work request calls take no flags argument of their own.
The flags that apply to an operation are those of the \f[CR]fi_op_*\f[R]
descriptor passed to fi_wr_prepare, and they carry the same meaning as
the flags of the corresponding fi_*msg call; see \f[CR]fi_msg\f[R](3),
\f[CR]fi_tagged\f[R](3), \f[CR]fi_rma\f[R](3), and
\f[CR]fi_atomic\f[R](3) for the flags usable with each operation type.
.PP
FI_MORE retains its meaning that further requests will follow, and the
deferred requests are initiated by the flush call for their queue
(fi_tx_flush, fi_recv_flush, or fi_trecv_flush).
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns 0 on success.
On error, a negative value corresponding to fabric errno is returned.
Fabric errno values are defined in \f[CR]rdma/fi_errno.h\f[R].
.SH NOTES
The work request calls described here are a host\-side interface.
A device\-side extension is described in \f[CR]fi_xpu\f[R](3).
.SH EXAMPLE
The following prepares an RMA write once, then repeats it to a set of
peers, changing only the destination address:
.IP
.EX
size_t wr_len = info\->ep_attr\->max_tx_wr_size;
fi_wr wr = malloc(wr_len);

\f[B]struct\f[R] fi_rma_iov rma_iov = { .addr = raddr, .len = length, .key = rkey };
\f[B]struct\f[R] iovec iov = { .iov_base = local_buf, .iov_len = length };
\f[B]struct\f[R] fi_op_rma rma = {
    .ep    = ep,
    .msg   = { .msg_iov = &iov, .iov_count = 1, .desc = &desc,
               .addr = dest_addr, .rma_iov = &rma_iov,
               .rma_iov_count = 1 },
    .flags = 0,
};
\f[B]struct\f[R] fi_wr_attr attr = { .op_type = FI_OP_WRITE, .op.rma = &rma };

ret = fi_wr_prepare(ep, &attr, wr, &wr_len);

\f[B]for\f[R] (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    fi_wr_modify_addr(ep, wr, FI_OP_WRITE, peers[i]);
    fi_wr_queue_tx(ep, wr, &contexts[i]);
}
fi_tx_flush(ep, 0);
.EE
